# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnp
.pnp.js

# Build output
.next/
out/
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Environment
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
!.env.example

# Local database (PGlite / Postgres data dir)
.data/
pgdata/
*.sqlite
*.db

# Raw dictionary sources — large, third-party, re-downloadable.
# See docs/DATA-SOURCES.md for provenance and how to fetch them.
data/sources/*

# ...except the owner's own corrections, which are not third-party material and are the
# input a re-import reads. They were living only on disk and in the image, so the rulings
# — which name is unisex, which spelling is anglicised — had no history and could not be
# reviewed. `data/sources/*` does not match below one level, so re-including the directory
# is enough; the raw scraped corpora stay ignored by their own rule.
!data/sources/names/
data/sources/names/scraped/
data/sources/names/owner/owner-list-*.txt
!data/sources/.gitkeep
!data/sources/README.md
data/derived/*
!data/derived/.gitkeep

# Third-party reference material, at ANY path.
#
# Corpus files get dropped into the workspace root by hand, where the
# data/sources/* rules above do not reach. That happened: 211 MB of scanned Edo
# dictionaries were staged for a push before anyone noticed. Two reasons this
# must never be committed — the scans are other people's copyrighted print
# dictionaries and redistributing them in a repository is not ours to do, and a
# 200 MB blob is permanent in git history even after deletion.
#
# Keep them on disk in data/sources/<language>/, which is ignored above.
*.pdf
*.djvu
*.epub
*.doc
*.docx
*.odt
*.rtf
# Scanned page images and audio corpora
*.tif
*.tiff
*.jp2
*.wav
*.flac

# Media
data/media/
# The WordPress export: 190MB of JSON regenerated by the importer (media.json, articles.json, and a page
# cache). Regenerable, so committing it would be wrong rather than safe — and NOT ignoring it meant a
# `git add -A` would stage the lot. Round 92 found the same one-command-away risk for a stray .env; this is
# the same shape, in the same directory tree, found by asking what else that command would sweep up.
data/ozikoro-wp/
*.mp3
*.wav
*.webm
*.ogg

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
logs/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp

# Scratch
.scratch/
/tmp-scratch/

# npm cache kept inside the workspace (the default ~/.npm may not be writable)
.npm-cache/

# Secrets
*.pem
*.key
!**/public/*.key

# Local tooling (AWS CLI venv etc). Never committed.
.tools/

# The TanStack Start learner app (github.com/idenze/paper-whisperer-forge).
# A separate stack with its own package manager; kept out of this workspace.
# Anchored: a bare `learn/` also matched the application at staging/learn/.
# The learner app at staging/learn/ is NOT excluded.
#
# It was, by a bare `learn/` rule, on the reasoning that it is a separate stack with its
# own package manager. The effect was that the entire application had NO version
# control -- 0 files tracked -- so a bad edit could not be reverted and nothing could be
# pushed. A separate stack still needs a history; if it should live in its own
# repository, that is a move to make deliberately, not a reason to leave it untracked.

# The design handoff's two demonstration PDFs are PART OF THE DELIVERABLE, not output.
#
# The blanket `*.pdf` rule above exists so generated documents and scraped PDFs never reach a commit. It also
# caught these two, in BOTH copies — the imported design source and the app's public/design — which meant a
# fresh clone received a design handoff missing two of its files while every check reported the handoff
# complete, because the checks read the working tree and the working tree had them.
#
# These are the screens' own download affordances: the Documents screen links them and the brief forbids
# treating them as official publications. They are small, static, and referenced by the design.
!apps/ozikoro/public/design/downloads/*.pdf
!design/calm-comfort-construct/public/design/downloads/*.pdf

# The design repository's COMPLETE git history, kept beside the files it belongs to.
#
# The working tree was imported file-by-file, which loses the 117 commits behind it. This directory is that
# history: a full clone's .git, readable with `git --git-dir=design/calm-comfort-construct/.git-upstream log`.
# It is ignored because it is history rather than source, and because its object files are not something the
# parent repository should carry twice.
design/calm-comfort-construct/.git-upstream/
apps/ozikoro/public/media

# The verify artefact a second build makes when the shared .next is in use.
# It is output, not source, and two builds sharing .next is how the review server was killed mid-write.
.next-verify/

# The build a `serve-review.sh` run stages while the live one keeps serving.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: `next build` empties its output directory before it writes, so building into `.next` takes
# the running server's files away at the first second of the build — measured, and the site answered 404 for
# eight minutes after one build that FAILED. `OZIKORO_DIST_DIR=.next-next` moves the build here, and the
# script swaps it into `.next` only once it is complete and its artefact has been asserted. Output, not
# source, and never committed.
.next-next/

# When the last `serve-review.sh` build began, which is what its freshness check compares sources against.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: the check compared against `.next/BUILD_ID`, which is written at the END of a build — so a
# source edited WHILE the build was running was never rebuilt, and every later run reported "the build is
# CURRENT" while the artefact held the pre-edit code. The marker sits beside `.next` because the swap's
# `rm -rf .next` would take a file inside it away with the build it describes. Plumbing, like `.next.lock`,
# and never committed.
.next.build-started

# The Igbo lexicon for the media service. DERIVED from the dictionary by
# `npm -w @ozikoro/media run lexicon`, and deliberately not committed: the owner's rule is that the
# dictionary lookup must query ozituma rather than read a list, because "a list would be stale within a
# week". The command and the coverage it prints are the committed artefact; the 0.5 MB snapshot is not.
apps/media/lexicon/

# Python bytecode from running the TTS worker. Compiled output, never source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch directories an agent may create when setting incomplete work aside.
# Their contents are recoverable from git history — they are never deliverables.
.incomplete-*/
.removed-*/

# The deploy lock — created and removed by scripts/deploy-to-host.sh
.deploy.lock
